# FlowEnergetic / DETONATION WAVE INTO ELASTIC COPPER, 1D  (Hydro2, energetic.on = 1, elastic.on = 1)
#
# Test 4.1 of Wallis, Barton & Nikiforakis, Comput. Struct. 254 (2021) 106578 (after Schoch, Nordin-Bates &
# Nikiforakis, JCP 252 (2013) 163):  copper for x < 0.15 m, idealised explosive for 0.15 < x < 0.4 m at rest and
# 1e5 Pa, the last 0.05 m of it (the booster) at 1e9 Pa.  The booster starts a detonation that runs to the left,
# reaches the copper and sends a shock into it; a shock returns into the products.  4000 cells.  SI units.
#
# Explosive (their Table 2, from Petitpas et al., Shock Waves 19 (2009) 377): reactant stiffened gas gamma = 4,
#   p_inf = 1 GPa, rho_0 = 1600, c_v = 2000; products ideal gas gamma = 3, c_v = 1000; Q = 3.68 MJ/kg;
#   d lambda/dt = -k sqrt(lambda), k = 2e6 1/s, where p > 1e8 Pa.
# DIFFERENCES FROM THE PAPER (stated, not tuned):
#   * mixture closure: reactant (phase 0) and products (phase 1) are two phases at one PRESSURE (six-equation model,
#     as Petitpas 2009); the paper's one-material mixture is at one pressure AND temperature.
#   * copper: the paper uses the Romenskii equation of state (rho_0 = 8930, K_0 = 136.5 GPa, G_0 = 39.4 GPa,
#     Gruneisen 2).  Hydro2 has the stiffened gas: gamma = 3 (= Gruneisen + 1) and p_inf = K_0/gamma = 45.5 GPa give
#     the same density, bulk modulus and Gruneisen coefficient at the reference state; shear modulus 39.4 GPa.
#   * boundaries (not stated in the paper): zero gradient at the copper end, a rigid wall behind the booster, so
#     that the products come to rest behind the Taylor wave and the total mass is conserved.
# Closed forms for this two-phase explosive (complete reaction, tests/FlowEnergetic/reference/detonation_check.py):
#   Chapman-Jouguet speed, pressure and the pressure of the products at rest behind the Taylor wave.
# Check:  python3 tests/FlowEnergetic/reference/detonation_check.py <run_dir>
